{"kind":"stack","id":"materials-uses","title":"Materials and their uses: metal, wood and plastic","theme":{"background":"#f4f9f4","foreground":"#1d3b2a","font":"Arial, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"image","id":"c1-p","picto":"coin"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"Why are saucepans often made of metal?"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a","label":"Metal conducts heat well and spreads it quickly across the base","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}],"why":"Category: a property of a material. Metals are good conductors, so the pan heats evenly."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Metal is always cheaper than any other material","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}],"why":"Pupils may think metal is cheap because it is common, but cost is not the test used for cooking."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-c","label":"Metal is easy to see through so you can watch the food","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}],"why":"Pupils may confuse metal with glass, which is transparent; metal is opaque."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Conductor"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"A material that lets heat or electricity pass through it easily, such as metal."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"image","id":"c3-p","picto":"tree"},{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Why is wood used to make a table top rather than a metal sheet?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Wood is warm to touch and does not feel cold like metal","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}],"why":"Category: a property of a material. Wood is a poor conductor, so it feels warmer than metal."},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Wood conducts electricity better than metal","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}],"why":"Pupils may reverse the rule: wood is an insulator, metal is the conductor."},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Wood is heavier than any metal of the same size","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}],"why":"Pupils may assume wood is heavy; many woods are lighter than metals."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"Which material is best for a raincoat?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Plastic, because it does not let water soak through","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}],"why":"Category: a material. Plastic is waterproof, so water runs off rather than soaking in."},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Paper, because it is thin and light","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}],"why":"Pupils may pick paper for lightness, but paper soaks up water and tears."},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"Wood, because it is hard and strong","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}],"why":"Pupils may confuse hardness with waterproofing; wood absorbs water and swells."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"image","id":"c5-p","picto":"ship"},{"type":"text","id":"c5-q","content":"You must build a boat. Will you use wood or metal?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-w","label":"Wood","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-m","label":"Metal","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"Your wooden boat floats well and is easy to repair. Well done. Continue to the next question."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"Your metal boat is strong but heavy, so it sinks slowly. Continue to the next question."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"Insulator"},{"type":"text","id":"c8-e","content":"A material that does not let heat or electricity pass through it easily, such as plastic."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"image","id":"c9-p","picto":"pencil"},{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Why is plastic used for the handles of pans and kettles?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Plastic is an insulator, so the handle stays cool to touch","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}],"why":"Category: a property of a material. Plastic does not conduct heat, so the handle stays cool."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Plastic is a conductor, so it passes heat along quickly","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}],"why":"Pupils may mix up conductors and insulators; plastic is the insulator."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Plastic melts easily when heated, so the pan is safer","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}],"why":"Pupils may think melting is a safety feature; a melting handle would be dangerous."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"In a fair test, which material kept an ice cube cold for longest when wrapped around it?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Wool, because it is a poor conductor of heat","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}],"why":"Category: a material. Wool traps air and slows heat moving in, so the ice melts less."},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Aluminium foil, because it is shiny","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}],"why":"Pupils may think shiny means insulating; metal foil conducts heat well."},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Steel, because it is very hard","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}],"why":"Pupils may link hardness with keeping cold; steel is a good conductor."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-t","content":"Well done: you can match materials to their uses."},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Keep testing and asking why. Scientists do exactly that."}],"actions":[]}}
